ReadMe

Class Name: RepairWorkshopConsole

Difficulty: Level 4 — Creative OOP Challenge

Estimated Time: 120–150 minutes

Actual Time Taken: 154 minutes

Written by: Chanwoo Park

Problem
Build a repair workshop application in which repair jobs protect their own
workflow state, standard and urgent jobs calculate charges polymorphically,
the workshop manages jobs through List<RepairJob>, and a storage class saves
and restores job data.

Program Flow
Create a workshop, register different repair-job types, execute valid and
invalid workflow actions, print filtered reports and status counts, save the
jobs, load them into a new collection, and print the restored data.

Class Responsibilities
- RepairWorkshopConsole: Coordinates the scenario, catches exceptions, and prints.
- RepairJob: Owns common data, validates state, and controls workflow transitions.
- StandardRepairJob: Calculates a standard final charge.
- UrgentRepairJob: Calculates a final charge with an urgent surcharge.
- Workshop: Owns the collection, searches jobs, and delegates actions.
- RepairJobStorage: Converts, saves, and loads repair-job data.
- JobStatus: Defines valid workflow states.
- InvalidWorkshopOperationException: Represents expected rule failures.

Requirements
- Invalid objects must never be created successfully.
- RepairJob must protect status-controlled fields without public setters.
- Workshop must delegate transitions and expose no mutable internal list.
- Business exceptions must reach the console instead of being printed internally.
- Charge calculation must use overriding and polymorphism.
- File operations must use try-with-resources and restore concrete subtypes.

Learning Objective
Practice object invariants, state ownership, exception propagation,
responsibility separation, polymorphic behavior, collection abstraction,
defensive copying, and file persistence in an open-ended multi-class design.
